   "text": "The pattern looks like two phases: Lu2PdO4 (mp-17662) plus Mg2CoWO6 (mp-1274182). The Lu2PdO4 assignment is fairly solid. The Mg2CoWO6 assignment is mostly by elimination.\n\n**Two peak families.** Converting the strong peaks to 1/d\u00b2 gives two series, each close to a simple pseudo-cubic perovskite lattice:\n- **Set 1 (a \u2248 3.91 \u00c5):** 32.3\u00b0 (the 100% peak), 46.4\u00b0, 57.9\u00b0, 67.5\u00b0 and 76.7\u00b0. These are also roughly consistent with 52.2\u00b0 and 53.0\u00b0.\n- **Set 2 (a \u2248 3.82\u20133.85 \u00c5):** 23.6\u00b0, 32.9\u00b0, 47.6\u00b0, 53.0\u00b0, 58.8\u00b0 and 74.7\u00b0.\n\n**Lu2PdO4 explains set 1.** Its conventional cell is about 5.533 \u00d7 11.65 \u00d7 5.531 \u00c5.\n- The Lu and Pd atoms form a nearly body-centred-cubic sublattice with a \u2248 3.9 \u00c5. That puts the strong lines near 32.35\u00b0, 46.4\u00b0, 57.8\u00b0, 67.7\u00b0 and 77.0\u00b0, which matches set 1.\n- A full-cell reflection near 52.9\u00b0 covers the 53.0\u00b0 feature. The 52.2\u00b0 peak is not accounted for by any candidate.\n- Its supercell reflections, such as the 24.0\u00b0 line, are weak: Lu and Pd contributions largely cancel, so they would sit near the noise.\n\n**Mg2CoWO6 is the best fit for set 2.**\n- Its cell volume per ABO3 unit is about 55.2 \u00c5\u00b3, giving a pseudo-cubic edge of about 3.81 \u00c5. Set 2 averages about 3.83 \u00c5.\n- Its roughly hexagonal axes give c/a \u2248 2.42. That is close to \u221a6, the value for a perovskite-like rhombohedral cell, so its main lines should",
